# tests/base/possess_lazy_guard.rxt — the 20 D47.6 lazy-possessification
# guard cells (Frank's ruling, docs/dev/decisions.md D47 ruling 6).
#
# Provenance: docs/design/eng_brep_design.md's [ENG-BREP] lane measured a
# repaired possessification analysis (§2.2) that a future implementation
# lane (module row [M4.6]) will use to compile some bounded/unbounded
# repeats WITHOUT backtracking machinery. The analysis carries a LAZY-only
# non-nullable-remainder conjunct (§2.3, [R24 S-F1]): a lazy loop whose
# remainder can match empty must NOT be possessified, because a lazy loop
# stops at the bottom of its exit chain where a greedy/possessive one tops
# out. `docs/design/eng_brep_measurements/probes/probe_possess.py` swept
# this conjunct and originally reported it declining 20 quantifiers the
# differential never showed diverging ("20 false declines") — refuted at
# D47.6: the probe's subject alphabet omitted the prefix byte `z` that these
# very 20 patterns are built from, so the "declines" were an artifact of an
# instrument that could not reach its own discriminating subjects, not a
# real cost. All 20 GENUINELY diverge lazy-vs-possessive once swept with a
# subject that actually has depth behind the prefix (see the probe's own
# fixed `subjects()` and docstring, and eng_brep_design.md §2.4/§10.2).
#
# THE POSSESSIFICATION PASS NOW EXISTS (src/opt/possessify.c, merged
# 2026-08-16 at 1eac1b9), so these cells are LIVE-FIRE, not anticipatory:
# every expectation below pins the lazy-quantifier behavior the shipped
# pass must preserve by DECLINING these loops. If the pass ever wrongly
# treats one of these lazy loops as possessive-equivalent, the compiled
# matcher would stop at the loop's maximal exit instead of its lazy
# (minimal legal) one, and the `disc` case below would fail. The
# `min`/`no` cases pin ordinary correctness (lazy loop takes exactly the
# floor at minimum-satisfying input; no match below the floor) so a
# regression can't hide behind "well the guard cell was contrived".
#
# Every case is oracle-verified three ways (D44): python3 `re`'s possessive
# quantifiers (3.11+) confirm the discriminating divergence itself (lazy
# spelling vs `+`-possessified spelling of the same base count) is real, and
# both python3 `re` and libpcre2 10.46 (options=0) agree with pcrec's own
# built output on every one of the 60 cells below, LAZY spelling only (the
# spelling actually written here) — verified via a one-off three-way sweep
# script (not committed; ad hoc per docs/testing.md's D44 rule), 0
# mismatches. The 6 capturing-group patterns (`z(a){...}`) get `g` lines for
# slot 1 (group 1), also oracle-verified against python's `match.span(1)`
# and libpcre2's ovector — both agree with pcrec's own capture slot.

pattern (?:ab){3,}?
m "ababab" 0 6
m "abababab" 0 6
n "abab"

pattern (?:ab){3,}?c?
m "ababab" 0 6
m "abababab" 0 6
n "abab"

pattern za{1,3}?
m "za" 0 2
m "zaa" 0 2
n "z"

pattern za{1,3}?c?
m "za" 0 2
m "zaac" 0 2
n "z"

pattern za{3,}?
m "zaaa" 0 4
m "zaaaa" 0 4
n "zaa"

pattern za{3,}?c?
m "zaaa" 0 4
m "zaaaac" 0 4
n "zaa"

pattern za+?
m "za" 0 2
m "zaa" 0 2
n "z"

pattern za+?c?
m "za" 0 2
m "zaac" 0 2
n "z"

pattern z(?:ab){3,}?
m "zababab" 0 7
m "zabababab" 0 7
n "zabab"

pattern z(?:ab){3,}?c?
m "zababab" 0 7
m "zabababab" 0 7
n "zabab"

pattern z(?:a|bc){3,}?
m "zaaa" 0 4
m "zaaaa" 0 4
n "zaa"

pattern z(?:a|bc){3,}?c?
m "zaaa" 0 4
m "zaaaac" 0 4
n "zaa"

pattern z(a){1,3}?
m "za" 0 2
g 1 1 2
m "zaa" 0 2
g 1 1 2
n "z"

pattern z(a){1,3}?c?
m "za" 0 2
g 1 1 2
m "zaac" 0 2
g 1 1 2
n "z"

pattern z(a){3,}?
m "zaaa" 0 4
g 1 3 4
m "zaaaa" 0 4
g 1 3 4
n "zaa"

pattern z(a){3,}?c?
m "zaaa" 0 4
g 1 3 4
m "zaaaac" 0 4
g 1 3 4
n "zaa"

pattern z(a)+?
m "za" 0 2
g 1 1 2
m "zaa" 0 2
g 1 1 2
n "z"

pattern z(a)+?c?
m "za" 0 2
g 1 1 2
m "zaac" 0 2
g 1 1 2
n "z"

pattern (?:z|)(?:ab){3,}?
m "ababab" 0 6
m "abababab" 0 6
n "abab"

pattern (?:z|)(?:ab){3,}?c?
m "ababab" 0 6
m "abababab" 0 6
n "abab"

# --- lazy + `$` follow (nested-lazy lane, 2026-08-16;
# outputs/nestedlazy_findings.txt Task B item 3). A bare `$` follow makes
# the remainder nullable in §2.2's sense REGARDLESS of (?m), so the lazy
# conjunct must decline these even though the greedy twin possessifies
# under the D47.5 `$` exemption. The discriminating subjects end in a
# newline: `$` also holds BEFORE a final \n, so a wrongly-possessified
# lazy loop would swallow the \n and report the longer span. Verified
# against python3 `re` and libpcre2 10.46 (both agree on every cell) and
# against the shipped pass's own stamp (0-of-1 possessified for the lazy
# spellings, 1-of-1 for the greedy control) at merge.

pattern [^c]{0,4}?$
m "ab\n" 0 2
m "ab" 0 2
m "abcde\n" 3 5

pattern ([^c]{0,4}?)$
m "ab\n" 0 2
g 1 0 2

# greedy control: the `$` exemption DOES possessify this one, and the
# possessified matcher must still take the full greedy span through the
# final newline — pins the exemption's soundness on the trickiest subject.
pattern [^c]{0,4}$
m "ab\n" 0 3
m "abcde\n" 3 6
